  3.873818253632366*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"856db0d6-cc73-47b1-91ea-\
c50353a455f8"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "According to ",
 ButtonBox["2101.04592",
  BaseStyle->"Hyperlink",
  ButtonData->{
    URL["https://arxiv.org/pdf/2101.04592.pdf"], None},
  ButtonNote->"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2101.04592.pdf"],
 " Table 1 at page 29. Note that the tetrad in the table is for Carter! So, \
need to convert to Kinnersley.\n\nChecked also manually that, for nm and nmb \
tetrad coefficients, we do find that at the level of the modes, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{
     SubsuperscriptBox["h", "nm", "lm"], "(", "r", ")"}], " ", "=", " ", 
    RowBox[{
     SuperscriptBox[
      RowBox[{"(", 
       RowBox[{"-", "1"}], ")"}], 
      RowBox[{"m", "+", "s"}]], 
     SuperscriptBox[
      RowBox[{"(", 
       RowBox[{
        SubsuperscriptBox["h", 
         RowBox[{"n", 
          OverscriptBox["m", "_"]}], 
         RowBox[{"l", "-", "m"}]], "(", "r", ")"}], ")"}], "*"]}]}], 
   TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->"4d74bb32-77ba-4141-a7ff-ee81d5d5c1e3"],
 ".\n\n",
 StyleBox["Note:",
  FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0, 0]],
 " this differs from the one in the numerically computed fourth order \
puncture below, where we exchanged the BLS indices 3 and 6, because the \
puncture (in all cases) is given in trace reversed form."
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{$Failed, 10}, {7, 8}},
 Evaluatable->False,
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.872755156919907*^9, 3.872755221618581*^9}, {
